ical personnel essential workers so  that they would have they could still  continue to serve the residents of New  York City and know that their children  would be taken care of that was a  surreal experience because now you have  children in a school environment so all  of the social distancing and the face  mask and the precaution and having 24/7  cleaning happening all the time and how  do you serve the food all of those were  the things that we were talking about  and planning for in addition we know  that eighty over eighty percent of the  students in New York City's public  school is qualified for free reduced  lunch so the poverty is real we know  that many of our students get their  meals at school so at the same time we  planned for and stood up over 450 meal  hubs across the city and every borough  where students could come get breakfast  lunch and dinner all in one fell swoop  and then of about three weeks ago we  extended that to not only children but  any resident can come to a meal hub and  get all three meals as well so we stood  that up as well because we know that  it's important for for students and  their their parents not to go hungry  I'll tell you this out right now since  we transition March 16th  we have served almost nine and a half  million meals out of those food hubs and  we're we're averaging right now a little  over half a million meals per day that  are being distributed and then we're  also doing other things we heard from  some of our students that said you know  during the school year we get feminine  hygiene products at our school for free  and you know there's a need for that so  we've started now distributing feminine  hygiene products that these meal hubs  again trying to be responsive to the  needs of our community and making sure  that they're being taken care of that's  that's incredible I can't even imagine  the complexity of doing all of that so  so quickly and we have a lot of comments  coming in you know one just from  F